title: Choosing: when and else
check: behaves
mode: vhdl
chapter: Choosing
---- intro
Often an output should be one thing or another, depending on a condition. VHDL says it almost in words:

```
y <= a when s = '0' else b;
```

"`y` is `a` when `s` is 0, else `b`." This part is a **multiplexer**, a switch that picks one of its inputs. A single bit is written in single quotes: `'0'` and `'1'`.
---- task
When `s` is 0, `y` should be `a`; when `s` is 1, `y` should be `b`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mux2 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    s : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end mux2;

architecture rtl of mux2 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity mux2 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    s : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end mux2;

architecture rtl of mux2 is
begin
    y <= a when s = '0' else b;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Use `when ... else`: one value when the condition holds, the other otherwise.
---- hint2
`y <= a when s = '0' else b;`
---- solution
`y <= a when s = '0' else b;` The drawing shows it as a multiplexer: `s` picks which input goes through.
---- done
That is the circuit you just drew with four gates, but `when ... else` says what you mean. VHDL lets you describe what a circuit does and works out the gates for you.
